			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1383843">
									

<p>Crows also remember individual humans who have annoyed them or displayed open hostility in some manner. <a href="http://news.discovery.com/animals/angry-crows-memory-life-threatening-behavior-110628.html">Interesting article on crow memory</a></p>
<p>My husband can attest to this. His parents' house (where we lived for quite a long time) is in the middle of rural, woodsy country. It's also right under a common crow route to and from nesting areas, so there's always a lot of crows around. DH's charming habit of hauling his bb gun out to take shots at them taught the entire crow population of the area that the minute they see the back door open, they're gone. But only when HE steps out. When I or anyone else steps out, they just hang about making noise.</p>
<p>Highly social birds are not nearly as dumb as people want to think. :)</p>

