														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1382594">
									

<p>I meant redactions in the more general sense, to include blackboxes, but those are gone now, so forget that part. As for tonal issues, these are all things that caught my eye personally. I use the imperative tense here a lot, so remember that it's just my opinion, which counts for whatever that's worth and no more.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Criticism</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Staff members should not be allowed to access them without being accompanied by security. Any staff members entering the enclosure must wear protective clothing covering the head and body.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I would replace references to &quot;them&quot; with SCP-1507 and &quot;the enclosure&quot; with the particular name/number/room/what-have-you of the enclosure. For that matter, I would replace the Wilderness Observation Cell in the containment procedures with something more specific, something like &quot;containment area 38 at Site 90210&quot; or similar.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If SCP-1507 appears to be vocalizing more frequently than is considered normal, the on-site soundproofing equipment must be activated until the vocalizations cease.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I assume you mean noise-cancelling speakers or something (I'm not an expert in acoustics). You might mention that such equipment must be present in this particular cell/area for containment purposes before saying that that equipment should be turned on should the situation require it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They are vulnerable</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I generally don't like the word &quot;they&quot; as used in this article; it seems less formal than the manner in which a researcher would be writing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-1507 was first discovered on 09/18/1991, in Jacksonville,Fl. They first came to the Foundations attention when a foundation followed up on a 911 call where a man claimed to be harassed by his lawn flamingos.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Space between comma and state abbreviation, remove &quot;they,&quot; don't use the word &quot;first&quot; twice in two consecutive sentences, use an apostrophe for possessive adjectives, and&#8230;actually, you might just rewrite the second sentence. &quot;when a foundation followed&quot; needs to be changed to whatever that phrase is supposed to say, &quot;a 911 call where a man&quot; is informal, and since &quot;where a man claimed to be harassed by his lawn flamingos&quot; isn't in quotation marks, it should use formal, clinical language as well (since it's a Foundation researcher summarizing an event in an official report).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Amended, see incident 1507-A and B</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Incident 1507-A and -B. Capital and period.</p>
<p>The incident log could use a reformatting, maybe put both logs in individual quote boxes rather than just their descriptions, and I would bold the headers.</p>
<p>Finally, the part where their principal attack is to use their metal stakes as claws just doesn't work for me, and it's kinda the most important part, since it's how they're able to attack people at all. I try to imagine how they fling themselves forward, spin around so that their stakes are pointing towards someone, and then&#8230;recoil-shoot the pointy part into someone? Or maybe bounce around and stab people that way? I dunno, but it isn't working for me as presently written. And there are probably more fixable spots in the tone; I just pointed out the ones that struck me the most.</p>
</div>
</div>
